# Whitelist. yadm's work tree is $HOME, so a plain `yadm add -A` or
# `yadm add .config/nvim` would sweep in whatever Omarchy ships next to these
# files. Ignoring everything makes both a no-op, and adding a file becomes a
# deliberate act: `dotfiles add <file>`, which passes -f. Tracked files are
# unaffected, so edits still show in `dotfiles status` and land with
# `dotfiles commit`.
/*
!/.gitignore

# Directories that are entirely mine. New files here need no -f.
!/.bashrc.d/
